1
00:00:00,907 --> 00:00:09,053
This is the full flow, not a mock-up, and it is explained step
by step. Four containers talk over real network calls: the

2
00:00:09,053 --> 00:00:17,334
payment bridge I built, a database, a simulator of a hotel PMS
in the style of the Oracle Hospitality Integration Platform,

3
00:00:17,334 --> 00:00:23,460
and a simulator of a card processor. Everything is synthetic.
First, the problem it solves.

4
00:00:24,096 --> 00:00:31,058
Why it exists. One hotel payment has to agree across three
systems: the PMS, the card processor and the guest's card.

5
00:00:31,058 --> 00:00:38,259
Things go wrong in five ways. A retry can charge a card twice.
A reply that arrives too late can post a payment twice. An

6
00:00:38,259 --> 00:00:45,399
outage can leave money charged but never on the folio. Card
numbers can leak into logs. And the books may not match what

7
00:00:45,399 --> 00:00:50,933
the processor settles. The bridge answers each one, and the
video shows every answer working.

8
00:00:53,092 --> 00:01:00,270
Who is who. Before the code, the people and systems in one
hotel card payment. The guest pays. The hotel's front desk

9
00:01:00,270 --> 00:01:07,877
runs a property management system, here OPERA, which holds the
guest's bill, the folio. In the middle is the payment bridge,

10
00:01:07,877 --> 00:01:15,361
the part I built. It sends the card to a payment processor.
Behind the processor are three parties the bridge never calls:

11
00:01:15,361 --> 00:01:22,662
the acquirer, the hotel's bank for cards; the card network,
such as Visa or Mastercard; and the issuer, the guest's own

12
00:01:22,662 --> 00:01:29,840
bank, which says yes or no. When the guest pays, the question
travels all the way to the issuer and back in about two

13
00:01:29,840 --> 00:01:37,079
seconds, and the guest's bank holds the money. The money
itself moves one to three days later, at settlement: from the

14
00:01:37,079 --> 00:01:44,379
issuer, through the network, to the acquirer, which pays the
hotel less the fees. In this demo, one simulator plays the

15
00:01:44,379 --> 00:01:46,956
processor and the three parties behind it.

16
00:01:48,469 --> 00:01:56,279
Here is the picture. A booking reaches the bridge as an event.
The bridge applies a payment rule and creates a payment link.

17
00:01:56,279 --> 00:02:03,334
The guest pays, and the card is tokenised the moment it
arrives: only a token comes back, and only ciphertext is

18
00:02:03,334 --> 00:02:10,514
stored. The bridge asks the processor to authorise and
capture, then posts the payment to the guest's folio in the

19
00:02:10,514 --> 00:02:18,261
PMS, and tells the hotel's own systems with a signed webhook.
Every step carries an idempotency key, and every failure ends

20
00:02:18,261 --> 00:02:25,693
in one of two places: posted, or kept in a dead letter queue
with the reason. Now let me show it, and then break it on

21
00:02:25,693 --> 00:02:26,197
purpose.

22
00:02:27,751 --> 00:02:35,010
First, the happy path. The PMS front desk takes a new
reservation and announces it to the bridge. Here the simulator

23
00:02:35,010 --> 00:02:42,582
posts the event over HTTP; real OPERA Cloud would deliver it
on OHIP's streaming API. The bridge answers with the payment

24
00:02:42,582 --> 00:02:50,029
rule: advance purchase, the full amount within twenty four
hours. Note the business date at the top. In a real property

25
00:02:50,029 --> 00:02:52,908
it moves only at night audit, not at midnight.

26
00:02:54,483 --> 00:03:01,951
In the bridge, a payment request now exists. The guest
tokenises a test card and pays. The card number is encrypted

27
00:03:01,951 --> 00:03:04,808
on arrival, and the guest sees only a token.

28
00:03:06,400 --> 00:03:13,575
On the processor side there is an authorisation and a capture,
and only the last four digits of the card. Behind the

29
00:03:13,575 --> 00:03:20,873
processor, the simulator also plays the card network, here
Visa, and the guest's bank, which approved with a code. The

30
00:03:20,873 --> 00:03:25,883
processor deduplicates on an idempotency key, so a repeat can
never charge twice.

31
00:03:27,407 --> 00:03:34,823
And at the front desk, the payment is on the guest's folio. I
open the reservation: folio window one, a payment code, a

32
00:03:34,823 --> 00:03:41,864
cashier, eight hundred dollars, our reference and the
processor's reference. It is a credit, posted as a payment,

33
00:03:41,864 --> 00:03:45,665
not as a charge, so the folio shows the stay paid in advance.

34
00:03:46,293 --> 00:03:54,179
The bridge also tells the hotel's own systems what happened.
Payment captured, and payment posted, each arrive as a signed

35
00:03:54,179 --> 00:04:01,936
webhook. The receiving side checks the signature before it
trusts the message, and every event has an id, so a repeat is

36
00:04:01,936 --> 00:04:04,522
ignored. The card number is never in it.

37
00:04:05,189 --> 00:04:13,050
Edge case one: the same booking event arrives again after the
guest has paid. The bridge does not create a new request, and

38
00:04:13,050 --> 00:04:20,209
does not ask for money twice. It flags the booking for a
refund or balance review, because a changed total after

39
00:04:20,209 --> 00:04:21,615
payment needs a human.

40
00:04:22,318 --> 00:04:29,878
Edge case two: a lost reply. This PMS does not deduplicate,
like the real world. I make it record the next payment and

41
00:04:29,878 --> 00:04:37,567
then answer too late. The bridge times out, but before it
retries it looks for its own reference, finds the payment, and

42
00:04:37,567 --> 00:04:41,155
does not post twice. One new line on the folio, not two.

43
00:04:41,851 --> 00:04:49,760
Edge case three: the PMS goes down after the card is charged.
The bridge says so plainly and keeps the payment in a dead

44
00:04:49,760 --> 00:04:57,999
letter queue with the reason. The money is not lost, and it is
not hidden. When the PMS is back, one click replays it, and it

45
00:04:57,999 --> 00:04:59,515
is posted exactly once.

46
00:05:00,765 --> 00:05:08,069
Edge case four: the processor approves the authorisation, then
declines the capture. The bridge does not leave the hold on

47
00:05:08,069 --> 00:05:15,134
the card. It releases it, and you can see the void in the
processor ledger. The payment link goes back to open, so the

48
00:05:15,134 --> 00:05:17,768
guest can try again, and this time it works.

49
00:05:18,391 --> 00:05:26,563
Edge case five: night audit runs and the business date moves,
and I also expire the access token. The next payment makes the

50
00:05:26,563 --> 00:05:34,471
processor capture the money and then go quiet. The bridge
fetches a new token, reads the new business date from the PMS,

51
00:05:34,471 --> 00:05:38,293
retries with the same key, and the processor charges once.

52
00:05:42,301 --> 00:05:49,660
At the end of the day, finance reconciles. The bridge
downloads the settlement file, what the acquirer paid out, and

53
00:05:49,660 --> 00:05:57,463
matches every capture against what was posted. Five captures,
five matches. Now a messy statement: one capture missing, one

54
00:05:57,463 --> 00:06:02,475
settled short, one we never made. Each exception has a name
finance can act on.

55
00:06:03,180 --> 00:06:10,368
The operator log shows all of this with the card number
masked. A mask catches mistakes, but the code never logs the

56
00:06:10,368 --> 00:06:16,875
number in the first place, and the vault view shows
ciphertext, never the number. Every call that changes

57
00:06:16,875 --> 00:06:24,374
something, every refusal, and every time a card is opened also
go to an audit trail, which the application refuses to run

58
00:06:24,374 --> 00:06:24,870
without.

59
00:06:26,449 --> 00:06:32,902
How do I know the other cases hold? Over five hundred
automated tests, and a coverage report above ninety nine

60
00:06:32,902 --> 00:06:39,942
percent of lines. The important tests were checked the hard
way: I put each bug back on purpose, over a hundred of them,

61
00:06:39,942 --> 00:06:46,864
and every one made its test fail. A page in the repository
lists every edge case and the test that fails if it breaks,

62
00:06:46,864 --> 00:06:48,566
and also what is not covered.

63
00:06:50,133 --> 00:06:58,166
How it should work in production. On AWS, the bridge runs as
two or more tasks in private subnets, behind a load balancer

64
00:06:58,166 --> 00:07:06,199
and a web application firewall, with PostgreSQL on RDS, keys
in KMS, and fixed outbound addresses for OPERA Cloud and the

65
00:07:06,199 --> 00:07:13,967
acquirer. The runbook is written, but not yet run in an
account. Booking events would come from OHIP's streaming API,

66
00:07:13,967 --> 00:07:21,934
which delivers at least once, with no acknowledgement and no
dead letter queue, so the bridge would store its offset and

67
00:07:21,934 --> 00:07:30,100
skip an event it has already seen. The processor becomes the
acquirer's checkout, with 3-D Secure and hosted card entry, so

68
00:07:30,100 --> 00:07:38,332
a guest's number never reaches this service. Authentication is
already in: OAuth2 tokens or hashed API keys, each limited by

69
00:07:38,332 --> 00:07:45,237
scope. Still to add: tenants for many operators and
properties, a sweeper for crashes between steps, and

70
00:07:45,237 --> 00:07:47,826
chargeback cases with scheme deadlines.

71
00:07:48,502 --> 00:07:56,758
That is the full flow: idempotent charges, a repeated event, a
lost reply, an outage and a replay, a released hold, a token

72
00:07:56,758 --> 00:08:04,611
refresh, and reconciliation, against separate systems. The PMS
here is a simulator I wrote in the shape of the Oracle

73
00:08:04,611 --> 00:08:12,666
Hospitality Integration Platform. It is not Oracle software,
and the real thing needs Oracle's own sandbox, which is the

74
00:08:12,666 --> 00:08:19,446
next step. The code, the tests and the log of what the agents
got wrong are in the public repository.
